66:\textbf{MoE on TSFMs collapses.} When we apply the standard MoE recipe~\citep{wang2022adamix, fedus2022switch}---routing on hidden states with load balancing---to a TSFM with internal RevIN, routing entropy drops to exactly $0.000\pm0.000$ in 7 of 12 unfrozen configurations (Table~\ref{tab:adamix}). The cause is \textbf{normalization-induced co-adaptation}: the dominant expert receives larger gradients, which reshape the unfrozen backbone to serve it better, completing a positive feedback loop within ${\sim}50$ steps (Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory}). Even when frozen, hidden-state routing underperforms because RevIN~\citep{kim2021revin} strips the temporal statistics the router needs. Applying RevIN to the router's input degrades MSE by $60$--$88\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:router_input}); Moirai~\citep{woo2024moirai} (no RevIN) does \emph{not} collapse (\S\ref{sec:cross_backbone}), confirming the failure is normalization-specific.
391:\label{tab:router_input}
425:\textbf{Quantitative prediction.} We define the \emph{routing signal ratio} $R(\mathcal{D}) = [\text{Var}(M) + \text{Var}(\Sigma)] / \overline{\text{Var}(S)}$. Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio} validates the prediction across all nine LTSF benchmarks: $R$ correlates with $\Delta\%$ at Spearman $\rho = -0.88$ ($p < 0.002$; 95\% bootstrap CI: $[-1.00, -0.51]$; permutation $p = 0.0032$; $n{=}9$). Traffic ($R{=}0.14$) is correctly predicted as a boundary case where raw routing provides no advantage ($\Delta{=}{+}2.9\%$). Solar ($R{=}0.06$) is the one outlier: the Conv1d router exploits temporal shape patterns (daily solar cycle) beyond the location-scale statistics that $R$ measures, consistent with the SSR ablation ($+43\%$ degradation when restricting to $\mu,\sigma$). The bound~(ii) \emph{generates} the prediction: high-$R$ datasets have small leakage $\varepsilon$, making the bound near-tight, while low-$R$ datasets have it vacuous---exactly the $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ pattern. Additional ablations---a sufficient-statistic router (SSR: $+43\%$ degradation), a router-detached gradient flow (RDGF: $+35$--$100\%$ degradation), and per-quartile routing analysis---are in Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}.
1001:The first term (self-gradient) always decreases $\ell_1$. The second (cross-gradient) is negligible when expert gradients are approximately orthogonal---a condition that holds empirically once experts specialize to distinct input clusters (Appendix~\ref{app:routing_weights}). The third (loss-coupling) term is small early in training when $|\ell_1 - \ell_2| \ll \|\partial\ell_1/\partial\mathbf{h}\|$. Retaining only the dominant first term:
1221:\label{app:routing_weights}
1257:\label{app:routing_ablations}
