\textbf{The Frozen Paradox.} Frozen RR-MoA beats \emph{full fine-tuning} (all 8 blocks unfrozen, best of 5 heads $\times$ 2 LRs) by $12$--$79\%$ on every dataset (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}, Figure~\ref{fig:frozen_paradox}). This comparison confounds architecture (MoE vs single-head) with freeze state, so it should be read as ``a frozen 5-expert MoE beats an unfrozen single-head adapter''---the single-head PEFT heuristic still holds ($1.254{\to}1.060$ on ETTh1, $-15\%$). Within RR-MoA itself, frozen is best on 4/6 datasets; on Weather and ETTm1, light unfreezing wins by up to $13\%$. Extended FT (50 epochs, cosine+warmup+layerwise LR decay) closes the gap by only ${\sim}4\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_ft}). Disabling RevIN recovers full-FT performance by $54$--$61\%$, confirming RevIN-induced signal loss is the mechanism.

\begin{proposition}[Gradient Co-Adaptation Drives Entropy Collapse]
\label{prop:frozen}
Consider a $2$-expert MoE with backbone $\mathbf{h}{=}A\mathbf{x}$, router $p{=}\sigma(\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{h})$, loss $\ell{=}p\,\ell_1{+}(1{-}p)\,\ell_2$. Under gradient descent on $(A,\mathbf{w})$: \textup{(i)} the dominant expert's loss decreases faster ($\dot{\ell}_k \propto {-}p_k\|\nabla_\mathbf{h}\ell_k\|^2$), creating self-reinforcing collapse toward $p{\in}\{0,1\}$; \textup{(ii)} freezing $A$ breaks this loop (proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}).
\end{proposition}

\textit{Remark.} Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} is for linear $\mathbf{h}{=}A\mathbf{x}$, but extends to non-linear networks via local Jacobian linearization. Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory}b confirms: per-expert gradient norms in a full 8-block Transformer follow the divergence pattern within ${\sim}50$ steps. \textbf{Routing vs.\ ensembling:} a fixed uniform mixture ($w_k{=}1/K$) is $30$--$75\%$ worse; independent ensembles (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}) are $37$--$46\%$ worse---confirming learned per-sample routing is the source of gains.

