\textbf{Why RevIN destroys routing: formal analysis.} Each input window $\mathbf{x}_i$ decomposes into mean $M_i = \mu(\mathbf{x}_i)$, scale $\Sigma_i = \sigma(\mathbf{x}_i)$, and shape $\mathbf{S}_i = (\mathbf{x}_i - M_i)/\Sigma_i$. RevIN maps $\mathbf{x}_i \mapsto \mathbf{S}_i$, discarding $(M_i, \Sigma_i)$.

\begin{proposition}[Routing Information Loss Under RevIN]
\label{thm:mi_decomp}
Let $X$ be a random input window, $E \in \{1,\ldots,K\}$ the expert assignment produced by any deterministic router, and $S = (X - M)/\Sigma$ the RevIN-normalized shape.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[\textup{(i)}] \textbf{Exact decomposition.} $I(X; E) - I(S; E) = I(M, \Sigma; E \mid S) \geq 0$.
    \item[\textup{(ii)}] \textbf{Unconditional quantitative bound.} Without any independence assumption,
    \[
        I(X; E) - I(S; E) \;\geq\; I(M, \Sigma;\, E) \;-\; I(M, \Sigma;\, S),
    \]
    where the residual $\varepsilon := I(M, \Sigma;\, S) \geq 0$ measures the ``leakage'' of stripped statistics through the shape.  Equality (tightness) holds iff $(M,\Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S$ given $E$.
    \item[\textup{(iii)}] \textbf{Full-information corner.} If the router depends \emph{entirely} on the stripped statistics ($E \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid (M,\Sigma)$), then $I(X; E) - I(S; E) = H(E)$: RevIN destroys the \emph{entire} routing distribution in nats.
    \item[\textup{(iv)}] \textbf{Empirical proxy.} The leakage $\varepsilon$ is empirically bounded: the maximum canonical correlation between $(M,\Sigma)$ and $S$ satisfies $\rho_{\max} \leq 0.50$ across all datasets, implying $\varepsilon \leq 0.15$ nats $\ll \log K = 1.61$ nats. The signal ratio $R(\mathcal{D}) = [\mathrm{Var}(M) + \mathrm{Var}(\Sigma)] / \overline{\mathrm{Var}(S)}$ then serves as a monotone proxy for $I(M,\Sigma; E)$, which increases with location-scale variance---making the bound~(ii) tight at high~$R$ and vacuous at low~$R$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

Part~(i) follows from the chain rule and RevIN's invertibility. Part~(ii) keeps the residual $\varepsilon$ explicit rather than assuming independence. Part~(iii) is the full-information corner. Full proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}.

\textbf{Quantitative prediction.} We define the \emph{routing signal ratio} $R(\mathcal{D}) = [\text{Var}(M) + \text{Var}(\Sigma)] / \overline{\text{Var}(S)}$. Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio} validates the prediction across all nine LTSF benchmarks: $R$ correlates with $\Delta\%$ at Spearman $\rho = -0.88$ ($p < 0.002$; 95\% bootstrap CI: $[-1.00, -0.51]$; permutation $p = 0.0032$; $n{=}9$). Traffic ($R{=}0.14$) is correctly predicted as a boundary case where raw routing provides no advantage ($\Delta{=}{+}2.9\%$). Solar ($R{=}0.06$) is the one outlier: the Conv1d router exploits temporal shape patterns (daily solar cycle) beyond the location-scale statistics that $R$ measures, consistent with the SSR ablation ($+43\%$ degradation when restricting to $\mu,\sigma$). The bound~(ii) \emph{generates} the prediction: high-$R$ datasets have small leakage $\varepsilon$, making the bound near-tight, while low-$R$ datasets have it vacuous---exactly the $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ pattern. Additional ablations---a sufficient-statistic router (SSR: $+43\%$ degradation), a router-detached gradient flow (RDGF: $+35$--$100\%$ degradation), and per-quartile routing analysis---are in Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}.

