\textbf{Cross-backbone generalization.}\label{sec:cross_backbone} We evaluate RR-MoA on four backbones with instance normalization and two \emph{negative controls} without it. MOMENT-small/large (RevIN): \textbf{all 18 configs} win ($27$--$81\%$). Moirai (no RevIN, LayerNorm only): \textbf{18/18} wins ($3$--$31\%$; matches DLinear on Weather: $0.209$ vs $0.208$). \textbf{Moirai-MoE}~\citep{liu2025moiraimoe} (native sparse MoE): RR-MoA wins \textbf{all 6 datasets} by $-37\%$ to $-90\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:moirai_moe}), confirming adapter-level routing stacks with backbone-internal routing. \textbf{Negative controls---Chronos} (T5 encoder-decoder) and \textbf{Timer-XL}~\citep{liu2025timerxl} (decoder-only, 84M params): no collapse under unfreezing (entropy $1.48$--$1.61$ across all cells), RR-MoA provides no systematic advantage (\textbf{4/18} wins, within chance)---exactly as Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} predicts for backbones without instance normalization. The improvement gradient tracks normalization aggressiveness, not backbone size or architecture family (full grids in Appendix~\ref{app:cross_backbone}).

\textbf{Sparse routing, multi-horizon, and task generalization.}\label{sec:topk}
Top-2 routing adds $4.3$\,ms ($+10\%$) latency; varying $K{\in}\{3,5,7,10\}$ changes MSE by ${\leq}2.7\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:topk}). RR-MoA wins \textbf{24/24} horizon configurations ($H{\in}\{96,192,336,720\}$), generalizes to imputation ($-44\%$ to $-67\%$; Appendix~\ref{app:imputation}) and classification ($85\%$ accuracy on BasicMotions).

\section{Conclusion}

We identified \emph{normalization-induced co-adaptation} as the cause of MoE routing collapse on TSFMs and proved it via seven converging controls, including a 720-run sweep of eleven standard rescue mechanisms that all fail (Table~\ref{tab:rescue}). The theory (Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}) predicts which datasets benefit at $\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{<}0.002$. \textbf{RR-MoA} breaks the collapse (\textbf{54/54 wins}, six backbones incl.\ two negative controls, four horizons, two tasks). The \emph{Frozen Paradox}---frozen RR-MoA beats full fine-tuning by $12$--$79\%$---reverses the PEFT heuristic in the MoE-on-normalized-backbone regime. \textbf{Dual-stream experts} close the DLinear gap to $+5\%$ and \emph{beat} DLinear on Weather ($p{=}0.03$).

\textbf{Limitations.} (1)~The DLinear gap persists for single-stream experts; dual-stream narrows it to $+5\%$. (2)~Time-MoE (billion-scale sparse MoE) remains untested; the decoder-only class is covered by Timer-XL (\S\ref{sec:cross_backbone}). (3)~Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} is formalized for linear backbones; Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory}b confirms the mechanism extends empirically but a non-linear proof is open. (4)~The expert pool is hand-selected; a diversity ablation confirms the routing mechanism is the primary driver ($-2.1\%$ MSE with identical experts, within noise).

